## ABSTRACT
Exploring alternative biomedical use of traditional drugs in different disease models is highly important as it can reduce the cost of drug development and overcome several critical issues of traditional chemodrugs such as low chemotherapeutic efficiency, severe side effect, and drug resistance. Disulfiram (DSF), a clinically approved alcohol-aversion drug, was recently demonstrated to feature tumor-growth suppression effect along with the co-administration of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ species, but direct $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ administration mode might cause severe toxicity originating from low $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ accumulation into the tumor and nonspecific $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ distribution-induced cytotoxicity. Based on the intriguing drug-delivery performance of nanoscale metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), we herein construct HKUST nMOFs as the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ self-supplying nanocarriers for efficient delivery of the DSF drug. The mildly acidic condition of tumor microenvironment initially triggered the release of Cu ions from HKUST nMOFs, which further reacted with the encapsulated DSF to form toxic $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{D D T C})_{2}$ (activation) for tumor chemotherapy. Especially, during the $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{D D T C})_{2}$ complexation, ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ species were formed concomitantly, triggering the intratumoral nanocatalytic therapy for the generation of reactive oxygen species to synergistically destroying the tumor cells/tissue. As a result, synergetic tumor-responsive chemotherapy and nanocatalytic therapy are enabled by DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs, as demonstrated by the dominant anticancer efficacy with satisfied biocompatibility both in vitro and in vivo. The present work offers a sophisticated strategy for tumor-responsive nontoxic-to-toxic therapeutic with high biocompatibility.
## KEYWORDS
disulfiram, metal-organic frameworks, chemotherapy, Fenton-like reaction, tumor suppression
## Introduction
Traditional chemotherapy has been clinically favorable treatment approach in cancer therapy for almost a century [1–3]. A variety of chemodrugs has been explored and developed as chemotherapeutic drugs for carcinoma destruction [4, 5]. In a large extent, chemotherapy has made a great step towards cancer healing. However, due to the intrinsic biological toxicities of most chemotherapeutic drugs, the non-tumor-site distribution into the human body is frequently associated with the intensified systemic side impacts, thereby limiting the ultimate therapeutic outcome against malignant tumors [6]. Targeting medications (e.g., Sorafenib) has been demonstrated to mitigate the undesired toxicities [7, 8], but their applications against tumors with such a variety are still challenging.
As the tumor cells progress and proliferate, these malignant tumor cells could adapt and develop several biological features that are distinct from the normal tissue cells. Typically, with the enhanced glycolysis process, the metabolic product of such a process lactate acid may eventually accumulate inside the tumor cells, constituting the mild acidic microenvironment with pH ca. 6.0 [9, 10]. In addition, with the exaggerated single-electron reduction reaction of oxygen molecules towards superoxide anions radicals in mitochondria, tumor cells upregulate the superoxide dismutase (SOD) to eliminate these radicals, simultaneously leading to the production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [11, 12]. With these two typical biological hallmarks, extensive tumor-responsive therapeutic modalities have been developed, including the acidity-responsive hydrogel drug delivery and nanocatalyst-based nanocatalytic therapy [13–15]. However, few reports have been focused on the design of tumor-responsive chemotherapeutics with the chemodrugs originating from other disease-corresponding therapeutic agents, which is of high significance because exploring new biomedical use of traditional drugs in different disease models can reduce the cost of drug development and overcome several critical issues of traditional chemodrugs such as low chemotherapeutic efficiency, severe side effect and drug resistance.
Disulfiram (DSF) is a US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved pharmaceutical agent for the treatment of alcohol withdrawal and chronic alcoholism in the past decades [16, 17]. Recently, pilot studies revealed that DSF molecules were featured with broad-spectrum anti-cancer activity against varied types of tumors including multidrug resistance tumor when co-administrated with copper gluconate [18–20]. Therefore, DSF is considered as an attractive drug candidate for tumor chemotherapy based on their intrinsic low-toxicity nature and tumor-suppression efficacy when conjugated with copper species [21, 22]. Regarding the copper-initiated toxicity of DSF, the $-S{-}S{-}$ bonding in DSF can be easily oxidized and chelated by metal ions, forming the cytotoxic $\mathrm{Cu}$ -diethyldithiocarbamate $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ moieties [23]. Yet up to now, the cytotoxic mechanism of $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ has not been fully revealed. Some studies evidenced that as an active species, $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ can effectively target some biomolecules (e.g., P-glycoprotein and NPL4 [24–26]) and inhibitor of tumor proliferation [27]. Within the cell homeostasis, copper species participated in many critical biological processes of cellular activities, in a dominant form of ceruloplasmin with an extremely low concentration of several micromolar $(5{-}25\ \upmu\mathrm{M})$ [26, 28]. However, the physiological concentration of copper may not be able to initiate the generation of sufficient $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ for explicit cytotoxic impact. Therefore, the introduction of exogenous copper supplying drug-delivery system would be appealing for such nontoxic-to-toxic DSF-related chemotherapeutics.
Nanoscale metal-organic frameworks (nMOFs) consisting of metal ion and organic ligands [29, 30] have attractive application prospects in biomedicine based on their high surface area and intriguing porous crystalline nanostructure [31–33]. Among these nMOFs systems, ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based HKUST nMOFs can degrade and release ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in acidic conditions, which has been explored in tumor-responsive suppression and bacterial-infection therapy [34, 35]. Based on the aciditytriggered Cu-releasing behavior and porous structure for drug delivery, it is highly expected that DSF could be efficiently encapsulated and released from ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based nMOFs, and further interact with nMOFs-supplying $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ to achieve enhanced DSF-based cancer chemotherapy.
In this work, DSF-loaded HKUST nMOFs (DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs) were carefully designed and elaborately constructed for highly efficient DSF-based cancer chemotherapy with tumor specificity. The PEGylation of $\mathrm{DSF}@\mathrm{HKUST}$ was conducted to guarantee the physiological stability of fabricated nanomedicine in body fluid. After in vivo intravenous (or intratumoral) injection, the $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs could effectively accumulate and localize inside the tumor region by the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect. Under the tumorous mild acidity, $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and DSF released from the integrated nanodrugs, which could lead to the in-situ formation of $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ by the interaction between ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and DSF and induce the tumor-responsive copper-activated chemotherapeutics. Furthermore, as the $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ formation was accompanied by the reduction process of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ into ${{\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}}.$ , nanocatalytic tumor therapy by the post-generated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ was enabled, during which ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ ions were able to catalyze the endogenously over-produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into hydroxyl radicals through Fenton-like catalytic reaction. Collectively, the designed copper self-supplying and DSF-loading DSF@HKUST nanodrugs could effectively induce the apoptosis cell fate against murine mammary 4-T1 tumor cells/xenografts both in vitro and in vivo by the tumor-specific, sequential and synergistic chemo/nanocatalytic therapy.
## 2 Results and discussion
The synthesized HKUST-based nMOFs were featured with prominent acidity-responsive degradation performance, leading to the release of DSF and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ species (Fig. 2(a)). Based on the interaction between ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and DSF, toxin substance $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ was in-situ generated, endowing cytosolic chemotherapy against cancer cells. In addition, during the formation process of $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}.$ , Fenton catalysis-active intermediate $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species were generated concomitantly, thereby activating Fenton-like chemical reactions to produce highly toxic hydroxyl radicals in response to the distinct tumor mild-acidic microenvironments. The acidity-responsive degradation profile of HKUST nMOFs was evaluated by TEM observation (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The synthetic HKUST nMOFs were physiologically stable upon the co-incubation within PBS in neutral condition. Only mild structural distortion of the HKUST nMOFs could be observed after the extended co-incubation time $(24\mathrm{~h})$ . Substantial framework destruction could be observed when HKUST nMOFs were incubated within acidic medium for just $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , suggesting that the degradation performance of HKUSTs nMOFs were especially acidity-responsive, providing possibilities for tumor-responsive therapeutics. The release behavior of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ species from HKUST nMOFs was evaluated in the phosphate buffer solution with different $\mathrm{\pH}$ values $\mathrm{\Phi_{pH}}7.4$ and 6.0) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under magnetic stirring (Fig. 2(b)). The HKUST nMOFs were relatively stable at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ according to the slowreleasing profile of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ species $52.7\%$ in $48\ \mathrm{h}$ ). In contrast, approximately $93.1\%$ of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ rapidly released from HKUST nMOFs under mild acidic condition $\left(\mathrm{pH}~6.0\right)$ within the identical period, sufficiently validating that HKUST nMOFs could gradually degrade in the mild tumor microenvironment. For DSF counterpart, the drug-releasing profiles from HKUST nMOFs were determined as well by ICP-OES under different pH conditions (Fig. 2(c)). It has been found that $47.34\%$ of DSF could be released at neutral PBS within $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , while approximately $82.48\%$ DSF was released under acidic environment within the identical duration. These results proved that both ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and DSF could be rapidly released from DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs in response to the mildly acidic tumor microenvironment. Subsequent formation of the bis(dialkylammonium)-tetrathiolanedication (Bitt- $4^{2+^{.}}$ ) leads to the simultaneous generation of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species through the two-electron reduction process [39]. Subsequently, $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ rapidly triggers DSF to form $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ and catalyzes $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into highly toxic •OH [40].
To further explore the ROS production performance of $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs, electron spin resonance (ESR) measurement was performed using 3,4-dihydro-2-methyl-1,1- dimethylethyl ester $-2\mathrm{H}$ -pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid-1-oxide (BMPO) to trap the possible generated hydroxyl radicals. As shown from the ESR spectra (Fig. 2(d)), no observable radical signals were detected for HKUST nMOFs, even in the presence of high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration $\mathbf{\dot{7}}\ \mathrm{mM})$ . Comparatively, the supplementation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $5\mathrm{mM}$ ) to DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrug dispersion yielded prominent •OH radical signals (characteristic peak intensity ratio of 1:2:2:1). Collectively for $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrug within the mild acidity and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ conditions, synergetic $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ formation and cascade $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ -mediated nanocatalytic radical generations were imparted, enabling the high potentials for carcinoma destruction both in vitro and in vivo.
To further evaluate $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ production in the presence of DSF, $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) was utilized to detect •OH generation efficiency in the following assay groups: $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ only, $\mathrm{DSF+H_{2}O_{2}}.$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}+D S F+H_{2}O_{2}}$ (Fig. S9(a) in the ESM). $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{DSF}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ groups showed lower activity after co-incubation with TMB for $5\mathrm{min}$ . Whereas, ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}+$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ mixed with TMB exhibited moderate •OH generation efficiency. Compared to the other groups, it could be indicated that the $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}+D S F+H_{2}O_{2}}$ assay group featured the highest chromogenic optical absorption at $650\mathrm{nm}$ , which was attributed to the hydroxyl radicals generated from the Fenton-like reaction in the presence of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ (Fig. S9(b) in the ESM).
The intracellular therapeutic process is summarized in Fig. 3(a). The cytotoxicity profiles of HKUST nMOFs, HKUST $\mathrm{nMOFs}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}.$ DSF and $\mathrm{DSF}@\$ HKUST nanodrugs at varied concentrations were investigated by the typical cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8) method. Upon co-incubation with HKUST nMOFs or HKUST nMOFs $+\ H_{2}O_{2}$ for $^{12\ \mathrm{h},}$ , satisfactory cytocompatibility could be seen at respective concentration of as high as $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Figs. S10 and S11 in the ESM). 4-T1 cancer cells treated with DSF (0 to $20\upmu\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ ) in the presence or absence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ $(20\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ ) remained high viability within an identical time-period, manifesting the non-toxic behavior of both HKUST nMOFs and DSF drug (Fig. 3(b) and Fig. S12 in the ESM). Comparatively, when the cancer cells were co-incubated with $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrug, the dose-dependent cytotoxicity profile could be monitored. The cell viabilities were only $42.01\%$ and $28.36\%$ of the control when the DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrug dosage reached $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ under neutral $\mathrm{(pH=7.4)}$ ) or acidic condition $\left(\mathrm{pH}=6.0\right)$ ), respectively (Fig. 3(c)). In addition, we demonstrated that acidic medium has no cytotoxic effect to cell viability (Fig. S13 in the ESM). The high therapeutic effect of the nanodrugs was specifically attributed to the acidity-enhanced DSF and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ species release upon the HKUST nMOFs degradation. These results proved that $\mathrm{DSF}@\$ HKUST nanodrug could significantly inhibit the proliferation of 4-T1 cancer cells, as compared to either DSF or $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ complexes [21]. As the supplementation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (as high as $500~{\upmu\mathrm{M}};$ contributed negligible cytotoxicity against the 4-T1 tumor cells (Fig. S14 in the ESM), we then investigated the nanocatalytic therapeutics of $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs by adding $200\upmu\mathrm{M}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for mimicking the tumorous intracellular microenvironment [41, 42]. With an identical dosage of DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs $(100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ , the cell viabilities were $35.24\%$ and $14.89\%$ of the control at neutral and acidic conditions, respectively (Fig. 3(d)), correspondingly higher than that of the $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs intervention group in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . These results evidently demonstrate that in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under the tumor microenvironment and with the acidity-responsive ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ degradation, DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs could enable synergetic $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ -based chemotherapeutics and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ -based nanocatalytic Fenton therapeutics, achieving effective carcinoma destruction with high cytocompatibility.
To indicate the cell fate of 4-T1 tumor cells after treatment of $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs, Annexin-V-FITC/PI staining assay was performed and quantitatively counted by flow cytometry (Fig. S15 in the ESM). It could be observed that cells upon $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs treatment showed substantial apoptotic cell population $(46.2\%)$ as compared to the other treatment groups. It is noted that in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ treatments of $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs could induce a higher percentage of cell apoptosis $(62.6\%)$ as compared to the treatment in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ , implicating that the cell death is specifically originated from the tumor-responsive copperactivated disulfiram synergized with the nanocatalytic tumor therapy.
To visualize the antitumor activity of DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs, Calcein-AM/propidium iodide (PI) staining protocol was used to indicate the viable and dead 4-T1 cancer cells with green $\chi_{\mathrm{Ex/Em}}$ : $488\ \mathrm{nm}/515\ \mathrm{nm},$ ) and red $(\lambda_{\mathrm{Ex/Em}};559\mathrm{nm}/580\mathrm{nm})$ fluorescence, respectively (Fig. 3(e)). After respective coincubation of HKUST nMOFs, DSF, or DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs with 4-T1 cancer cells (both in neutral and acidic conditions) for $^{\mathrm{~4~h~}}$ , HKUST nMOFs and DSF treated tumor cells displayed strong green fluorescence inside the cells, indicating the absence of cytotoxicity. Comparatively, the DSF@HKUST treatment group exhibited a majority of red fluorescent cancer cells, illustrating the substantially higher cytotoxicity than that of the HKUST nMOFs or DSF treatment groups. Comparing the cells treated under the neutral and acidic conditions, it has been found that a higher proportion of dead cells was presented in acidic condition for $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST+H_{2}O_{2}}$ treatment group, which showed consistent results to the in vitro cytotoxicity profiles. Therefore, the acidic tumorous microenvironment responsive cytotoxicity of DSF $@$ HKUST nanodrugs was evidently conceived.
To further confirm that $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ could elevate the intracellular ROS in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ 2,7-dichlorofluorescein diacetate (DCFH-DA) was employed to detect the intracellular ROS level through direct confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) observation. After DCFH-DA staining of the co-incubated 4-T1 tumor cells with HKUST nMOFs, DSF, DSF@HKUST and $\Delta\mathrm{SF}@\mathrm{HKUST~+~H_{2}O_{2}}$ respectively, the intracellular DCFH-DA could be oxidized to fluorescent DCF upon the interaction with intracellular ROS. It could be found that cells incubated with either DSF or HKUST nMOFs exhibited negligible green fluorescence under both acidic and neutral conditions, indicating the extremely low intracellular ROS-generation capability (Fig. 3(f)). On the contrary, a large amount of intracellular ROS was generated in tumor cells treated with $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ . With $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ supplementation, the total fluorescence intensity was elevated. It is noted that for $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrug and nanodrug $+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ treatment group co-incubated in acidic medium, intensified DCF fluorescence intensity was observed. These results further confirmed the tumorous mild-acidic degradation of DSF $@$ HKUST, subsequent $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2}$ -based chemotherapeutics and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ -based nanocatalytic therapeutics against tumor cells.
As shown in Fig. 4(f), H&E, antigen Ki-67 and TdT-mediated dUTP nick-end labeling (TUNEL) staining of the dissected tumor xenografts were performed to indicate the pathological status of the tumor cells. According to the H&E and TUNEL staining images of the tumor slices, a large proportion of tumor cells was destructed upon treatments of $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs, as compared to the single treatments of saline or DSF. Correspondingly, fewer Ki67-positive nucleus was observed when treated with $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs, indicating the less proliferation property (Fig. S18 in the ESM). In addition, after evaluating the pathological damage of the major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney) of mice by H&E staining, satisfactory in vivo biocompatibility was validated during the 14 days therapeutic investigations. No noticeable variations were monitored between control and treatment groups (Fig. S19 in the ESM). Collectively, these results solidly demonstrated the highly efficient and biologically safe synergistic therapeutic effect of $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs, as contributed by the tumorous acidity-responsive self-supplying $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ HKUST nMOFs and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -activated DSF-based chemotherapeutics and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ -initiated nanocatalytic therapeutics.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, a Cu self-supplying and DSF-loading nanodrug $({\mathrm{DSF}}@{\mathrm{HKUST}})$ ) was constructed for achieving tumor-specific, sequential and synergistic chemo activation and nanocatalytic tumor therapy. It has been demonstrated that under the tumor acidic microenvironment, the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and encapsulated DSF chemodrug were quickly released by nanocarrier degradation, enabling the interaction of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and DSF agents to form cytotoxic chemotherapeutic $\mathrm{Cu(DDTC)}_{2},$ as well as the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}.$ - initiated nanocatalytic Fenton therapeutics. At the intracellular level, 4-T1 tumor cells were effectively destructed by the synergetic therapeutics of $\mathrm{DSF}@$ HKUST nanodrugs in a tumor-responsive manner, which was solidly evidenced by versatile characterization techniques. In addition to the high in vivo biocompatibility, both intratumoral and intravenous injections of $\mathrm{DSF@HKUST}$ nanodrugs exerted prominent tumor-suppression effects for 4-T1 tumor xenografts as compared to the neglectable therapeutic effect of free DSF drug. This paradigm provides a synergetic tumor-responsive chemotherapeutic and nanocatalytic therapeutic modality, which also paves a new way for achieving non-toxic to toxic transformable nanomedicine based on features of the tumor microenvironment and specific interaction between the carrier and loaded chemotherapeutic agents.
